{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for served MCP stage 0 at 6abe1ce86a505ebc6ee5d47a7875f10187ea2c17, resting on all 55 artifacts with notes/mcp-stage0.md first.\n\n[P2] The new live harness can report successful cleanup when resources remain. In packages/room/measure/mcp-stage0.mjs, cleanup treats unsuccessful inventories as empty, does not validate list identities/completeness, and the finalizer ignores cleanup.error and individual token revocation/deletion results. Seven local controls execute the candidate's actual cleanup and finalizer with a fake API. Healthy cleanup exits 0 and a refused deletion followed by a known-present inventory exits 1. But refused inventory, thrown inventory, malformed [{}] inventory, refused token revocation, and refused deletion followed by an unknown inventory each produce ok:true and exit 0. In the unknown/throw/malformed cases the canonical repository and write credential remain. An exception while cleaning one agent can also terminate cleanup with later resources unattempted. Use the reviewed deploy V3 cleanup rules or a shared validated helper: retain known canonical/fork/token identities before effects, treat unknown/incomplete/malformed inventories as unresolved, attempt independent cleanup despite failures, record each outcome, and return nonzero whenever any duty remains unresolved. Test those branches against this harness's real finalizer. This finding does not assert that the recorded successful runs leaked resources.\n\nThe combined candidate also carries the three founding blockers in effective review a35b4b61987b19e97c95ed3597637eaff537594f. Those source bytes remain unchanged from d8312c32: an unknown canonical mint can be retired before its delayed effect; canonical token cleanup ignores incomplete inventories; configured-but-missing import bindings can leave registry bindings after failed founding. Integrate the corrected founding delivery once independently reviewed. Revision a5185fa6 is now queued; its existence does not approve these inherited bytes. Preserve the current main's bounded-memory publisher and deploy V3 cleanup as well. A local merge preview against main 9d0d0cbd shows a real conflict in measure/spike-smoke.mjs, so the resolved combined head needs review.\n\nArchitecture: the endpoint is a small adapter over the existing client, RoomWire, bearerAct and bearerRequest. It adds no second admission authority and serves the full Room handle for later read tools. The anchored route preserves other HTTP paths. The official client passes in both MCP 2026-07-28 and legacy stateless modes. All ten tools advertise object-rooted schemas; eight refusing tools advertise result/Refusal oneOf. Omitting Refusal from attention/explain is a reasonable scoped interpretation because those reads do not return Refusal. Titles/annotations are explicitly deferred by the implementation request; the amendment's four added read tools and 45-second wait cap remain separate work. The observed 60-second cold-agent land call is not evidence of that future cap.\n\nSecurity: ordinary acts are signed under the bearer delegation and checked by the Room, including reads and workspace grants. Three additional real-DO/workerd controls passed: expiry gives 401 without appending a tool act; another member cannot obtain the owner's ready workspace token; foreign Origin and Host requests give 403 without admission. My first version of these controls omitted the matching Host header in the synthetic workers.dev request; correcting that fixture made all three pass. This was not a candidate defect. Tokens were not exposed in these control responses or the saved evidence. The new cleanup uncertainty is the security blocker.\n\nSimplification: reusing the existing bearer client and stateless handler keeps the endpoint comprehensible. Reusing the validated cleanup implementation will also avoid maintaining inconsistent proof-of-cleanup rules in two measurement scripts.\n\nValidation: fresh npm ci installed 374 packages; root typecheck passed; root tests passed 1,621 with one existing skip. Git's additional workerd and bounded-log gates passed 8 and 11 tests. Both Room dry deployments passed at 1,531.97 KiB (334.90 KiB gzipped). Read all 55 artifacts: 33 have exact byte credit from previously read founding/base heads and 22 received new source/diff/evidence review; the manifest exactly matches changed paths against 3f44c993. Parsed both dated live results and all 47 cold-agent transcript records. The cold agent's propose/land/release receipts match main 5e5b699a51a3640768282232cafab4fa8c6a7dad; the empty default released-lane listing alone proves nothing. Those runs support the deployed happy path, not failure cleanup or complete MCP conformance. No live deployment, credentials, repository deletion or model invocation was performed for this review.\n\nDiagnostics and gate logs are under /tmp/artroom-checker-mcp-stage0-diagnostics and /tmp/artroom-checker-mcp-stage0-*. The candidate worktree is clean. Advertised structured results were assessed against the official tools specification: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/server/tools .","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0011cae195dd30333c6cb3a6c9434f9ff2b9663e","binding":"assigned","head":"6abe1ce86a505ebc6ee5d47a7875f10187ea2c17","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:96d6b6c17ca27fa6b5b0a7c63c406af44fd9d616\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a8436f54f6a775ae6bd0bf1b97b4985be350e13e\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ae33d4e09e07a68da969e9ae612873357a331d71","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}